# Speedat - web-root .htaccess (Hostinger public_html).  DEPLOYMENT REFERENCE COPY.
#
# WHICH FILE IS LIVE
#   The release built by bin/build-release.php ships php/public/.htaccess as
#   public_html/.htaccess. THIS file is the annotated reference: same rules,
#   plus the blocks that are deliberately switched off by default (HSTS, the
#   http->https redirect, ModSecurity relief). Paste from here through hPanel ->
#   File Manager when something has to be turned on on the host; do not let the
#   two drift silently - build-release.php prints which file it shipped.
#
# WHY IT IS SO DEFENSIVE
#   app/, config/, storage/, db/, bin/ and var/ are SIBLINGS of public_html, so
#   nothing sensitive is under a URL at all. Every deny rule below is the second
#   lock: it only matters on the day somebody copies the tree into the web root
#   or a control panel "repairs" the document root.
#
# HOSTINGER REALITY (see docs/hostinger-constraints.md)
#   The server is LiteSpeed, not Apache. .htaccess is the ONLY configuration
#   surface and it is re-read per request - no restart, but also no error other
#   than a hard 500 if a directive is refused. So:
#     * EVERY mod_* block is wrapped in <IfModule>. A missing module then
#       degrades quietly instead of taking the whole site down.
#     * No <Directory>, no <VirtualHost>, no php_admin_value - all invalid or
#       refused here.
#     * Change this file in small, reversible steps and reload the site after
#       each one.

Options -Indexes -MultiViews
DirectoryIndex index.php

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 0. The web server's own body cap
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2 MiB, refused a layer EARLIER than everything else on this site.
#
# post_max_size (section 6, and .user.ini) stops a giant body before the
# application sees it, but PHP still has to be started, handed the request and
# asked to read it. LimitRequestBody is the web server refusing the body with a
# 413 before a PHP worker is engaged at all, which on a one-core shared plan is
# the difference between a refusal costing nothing and a refusal costing a
# worker for as long as the sender cares to keep sending.
#
# THE SIZE IS FOR A SITE WITH NO UPLOADS, WHICH IS WHAT THIS SITE IS. Not one
# form carries enctype="multipart/form-data" - the Excel rate sheet was removed
# (owner, 2026-09-27) - so the largest honest request is the rates editor's few
# hundred text fields, tens of kilobytes. 2 MiB is roughly fifty times that and
# sits UNDER post_max_size (3M) on purpose: whichever layer a host honours, the
# web server's cheaper refusal fires first and the PHP-side cap stays the
# backstop rather than the front line.
#
# WHY IT IS WRAPPED, WHEN LimitRequestBody IS CORE APACHE AND NOT A mod_*.
# Because the rule at the top of this file is absolute: an unsupported
# directive here is a hard 500 that takes the whole site down, and "core
# Apache" is a statement about Apache, not about LiteSpeed's .htaccess subset.
# `core.c` is the module identifier Apache itself answers to. A host that does
# not know that name gets the second block, which asks for the same cap on any
# server already proving it parses <IfModule> by giving us mod_rewrite -
# without which this site has no front controller and serves nothing at all.
#
# A host matching NEITHER sets no cap here and does not 500, and post_max_size
# in .user.ini still refuses the body. That is the correct direction to fail:
# a missing optimisation, never a missing site.
#
# IF A LEGITIMATE POST EVER 413s, raise this number AND post_max_size together,
# keeping this one the smaller of the two. tests/Core/UserIniTest.php fails if
# they cross over.
<IfModule core.c>
    LimitRequestBody 2097152
</IfModule>
<IfModule !core.c>
    <IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
        LimitRequestBody 2097152
    </IfModule>
</IfModule>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Front controller
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
    RewriteEngine On
    RewriteBase /

    # Never rewrite something that really exists on disk (assets, index.php).
    RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} -f [OR]
    RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} -d
    RewriteRule ^ - [L]

    # Everything else is the application's problem.
    RewriteRule ^ index.php [L]

    # --- OPTIONAL: force https ---------------------------------------------
    # LEAVE THIS OFF on Hostinger. Free certificates are installed
    # automatically and the platform already forces https; adding a second
    # redirect here gives every visitor two round trips, and gets it wrong
    # behind a proxy that terminates TLS (%{HTTPS} reads "off" on the origin
    # even though the browser is on https, which is an infinite redirect).
    #
    # Turn it on ONLY if you have verified with curl -I that a plain http://
    # request is answered 200 instead of 301. Then uncomment all three lines.
    #
    # RewriteCond %{HTTPS} !=on
    # RewriteCond %{HTTP:X-Forwarded-Proto} !=https
    # RewriteRule ^ https://%{HTTP_HOST}%{REQUEST_URI} [R=301,L]
</IfModule>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Deny everything that is not meant to be downloaded
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both syntaxes are present because Apache 2.2 and 2.4 disagree and a shared
# host is not always the version its panel claims.
<IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
    # Dotfiles: .env, .env.local, .git*, .htpasswd, .user.ini, editor droppings.
    # .htaccess itself is already protected by the server; denying it here as
    # well costs nothing.
    <FilesMatch "^\.">
        Require all denied
    </FilesMatch>

    # Source, data and configuration extensions. A .sql in the web root is a
    # database dump, and a database dump of this site contains customer leads.
    <FilesMatch "\.(sql|sqlite|sqlite3|db|md|markdown|log|ini|yml|yaml|lock|sh|bash|ps1|bak|old|orig|swp|dist|sample|tpl|twig|ts|tsx|mjs|cjs|map|zip|gz|tar|7z|rar|pem|key|p12|pfx|crt|csr)$">
        Require all denied
    </FilesMatch>

    # ALL .json. Nothing this site serves is .json-as-a-file: the API answers
    # are generated by index.php and have no extension. If a web manifest is
    # ever needed, name it site.webmanifest (which is the correct name anyway)
    # rather than poking a hole in this rule.
    <FilesMatch "\.jsonc?$">
        Require all denied
    </FilesMatch>

    # Names that are a leak even with a harmless extension.
    <FilesMatch "^(composer|package|package-lock|pnpm-lock|phpunit|Dockerfile|docker-compose|\.?env)">
        Require all denied
    </FilesMatch>

    # The PHP built-in-server router. Never used under LiteSpeed/Apache, and
    # build-release.php excludes it, but it is one line of insurance.
    <Files "router.php">
        Require all denied
    </Files>
</IfModule>

<IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
    <FilesMatch "^\.|\.(sql|md|log|ini|yml|yaml|lock|sh|bak|dist|sample|jsonc?|pem|key)$">
        Order allow,deny
        Deny from all
    </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

# Refuse the HTTP methods this site has no use for. WebDAV verbs in particular
# are a scanner favourite and index.php would answer 404 to them anyway - this
# stops them before PHP starts.
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
    RewriteCond %{REQUEST_METHOD} ^(TRACE|TRACK|PROPFIND|PROPPATCH|MKCOL|COPY|MOVE|LOCK|UNLOCK|PATCH|PUT)$
    RewriteRule ^ - [F,L]
</IfModule>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Security headers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# mod_headers on Hostinger is VERIFY - LiteSpeed supports it, Hostinger never
# confirms it. Check once with curl -I after the first deploy; if the headers
# are missing, the application sets the same ones from PHP and nothing breaks.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
    Header always set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
    # DENY, matching app/Core/App.php and the CSP's `frame-ancestors 'none'`.
    # Nothing on this site is framed by anything, so there is no same-origin
    # embed to keep working and no reason for this header and the CSP to hand
    # a browser two different answers to the same question.
    Header always set X-Frame-Options "DENY"
    Header always set Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
    Header always set Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy "same-origin"
    Header always set Permissions-Policy "geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=(), usb=()"

    # HSTS. OFF until the PHP site IS the live https site. A browser remembers
    # this for a year: set it on a domain that still has to answer plain http
    # for anything and you have locked yourself out of your own domain for
    # twelve months. No `preload` until the site has run cleanly for weeks.
    # Header always set Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"

    # PHP's version banner. expose_php is also Off in .user.ini.
    Header always unset X-Powered-By
    Header always unset Server

    # Never let a proxy or the browser cache an admin page or an API answer.
    # index.php sets Cache-Control itself; this is the belt to that braces.
    <FilesMatch "^index\.php$">
        Header always set Cache-Control "no-store, no-cache, must-revalidate, max-age=0"
    </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. MIME and compression
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
<IfModule mod_mime.c>
    AddDefaultCharset UTF-8
    AddType font/woff2 .woff2
    AddType image/svg+xml .svg
    AddType application/manifest+json .webmanifest
</IfModule>

<IfModule mod_deflate.c>
    AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE text/html text/plain text/css text/javascript \
        application/javascript application/json image/svg+xml \
        application/manifest+json
    # Do NOT compress fonts or images: woff2, png, jpeg and webp are already
    # compressed, and gzipping them costs CPU on a 1-core plan for nothing.
</IfModule>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Static asset caching
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HTML is never cached. Prices change on publish and a cached page is a wrong
# price - the one bug this whole project exists to avoid.
#
# Assets are cached hard because every reference to them carries a
# ?v=<filemtime> query string (there is no build step to hash filenames), so a
# changed file is a changed URL.
<IfModule mod_expires.c>
    ExpiresActive On
    ExpiresDefault "access plus 0 seconds"
    ExpiresByType text/html "access plus 0 seconds"
    ExpiresByType text/css "access plus 7 days"
    ExpiresByType text/javascript "access plus 7 days"
    ExpiresByType application/javascript "access plus 7 days"
    ExpiresByType image/svg+xml "access plus 30 days"
    ExpiresByType image/webp "access plus 30 days"
    ExpiresByType image/png "access plus 30 days"
    ExpiresByType image/jpeg "access plus 30 days"
    ExpiresByType image/x-icon "access plus 30 days"
    ExpiresByType font/woff2 "access plus 365 days"
</IfModule>

<IfModule mod_headers.c>
    # immutable tells the browser not even to revalidate. Safe only because of
    # the ?v= cache-buster above.
    <FilesMatch "\.(css|js|woff2|svg|png|jpe?g|webp|ico)$">
        Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=604800, immutable"
    </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. PHP settings, if the host lets .htaccess set them
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hostinger documents php_value in .htaccess as working (PHP runs through
# LSAPI). It is still wrapped, and nothing in the application depends on these
# being applied - the real place to set them is hPanel -> PHP Configuration, or
# the .user.ini shipped beside this file.
#
# 2M / 3M, and the smallness is the point. Nothing on this site uploads a file
# any more (the Excel rate sheet was removed, owner 2026-09-27); the largest
# legitimate body is the rates editor's text fields, tens of kilobytes. A 12 MB
# POST at the contact form has to be refused BEFORE PHP hands it to the
# application, and this is the only layer that can do that. post_max_size stays
# 1M above upload_max_filesize so that a body at exactly the file limit is not
# silently discarded whole.
#
# max_input_vars 4000, the same number the .user.ini beside this file sets. It
# has to be the same: a host reads one or the other and never announces which,
# so two different numbers mean the live limit is an accident of the SAPI. The
# largest form on the site is one country row in the rates editor - 98 named
# controls, measured 2026-09-29 against the real document, with form#editor at
# 48 behind it - so 4000 is about forty times the worst honest case. The margin
# is deliberate: max_input_vars counts intermediate array dimensions too, a
# cargo threshold or a fourth service multiplies a row, and a body PHP cut short
# is not an error - $_POST just ends early. Http\Admin\DraftForm refuses a save
# that arrived short, so this is headroom rather than something depended on.
<IfModule mod_php.c>
    php_flag display_errors Off
    php_flag expose_php Off
    php_value upload_max_filesize 2M
    php_value post_max_size 3M
    php_value max_input_vars 4000
</IfModule>
<IfModule lsapi_module>
    php_flag display_errors Off
    php_flag expose_php Off
    php_value upload_max_filesize 2M
    php_value post_max_size 3M
    php_value max_input_vars 4000
</IfModule>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. OPTIONAL: ModSecurity relief for the rates admin
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ModSecurity is ON by default on Hostinger and false-positives on exactly what
# a rates admin posts: long text, numbers in SQL-ish shapes, pasted HTML. The
# symptom is a 403 that never reaches PHP, so it is absent from storage/logs.
#
# Do NOT uncomment this pre-emptively. If and only if a specific admin POST
# 403s while the same request succeeds with a shorter body, narrow the rule to
# that ONE path - never the whole site.
#
# <IfModule mod_security.c>
#     <Location "/admin/rates">
#         SecRuleRemoveById 200003
#     </Location>
# </IfModule>
#
# (<Location> is invalid in .htaccess. On Hostinger the supported route is
#  hPanel -> Advanced -> ModSecurity, which toggles it per directory. Put the
#  admin under its own directory only if it comes to that.)
